{"t": " and", "lp": -0.5856435894966125, "top": [[" and", -0.5856435894966125], [",", -1.0028475522994995]]}, {"t": " I", "lp": -0.7972345948219299, "top": [[" I", -0.7972345948219299], [" specific", -1.427052617073059]]}, {"t": "'m", "lp": -1.0482029914855957, "top": [["'m", -1.0482029914855957], [" could", -1.4704957008361816]]}, {"t": " not", "lp": -0.0014699767343699932, "top": [[" not", -0.0014699767343699932], [" somewhat", -7.882775783538818]]}, {"t": " ", "lp": -0.03690164163708687, "top": [[" ", -0.03690164163708687], [" entirely", -4.493623733520508]]}, {"t": "1", "lp": -0.000971431378275156, "top": [["1", -0.000971431378275156], ["0", -10.82332706451416]]}, {"t": "0", "lp": -0.0008147100452333689, "top": [["0", -0.0008147100452333689], [",", -11.086915969848633]]}, {"t": "0", "lp": -0.0007152134203352034, "top": [["0", -0.0007152134203352034], ["1", -10.146601676940918]]}, {"t": "%", "lp": -6.568647222593427e-05, "top": [["%", -6.568647222593427e-05], ["%%", -10.76148509979248]]}, {"t": " certain", "lp": -0.0008984070154838264, "top": [[" certain", -0.0008984070154838264], [" sure", -8.202064514160156]]}, {"t": " of", "lp": -0.1392102688550949, "top": [[" of", -0.1392102688550949], [" I", -2.5315613746643066]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- The driver uses hardware preemption to manage compute contexts\n- CCS contexts are allocated dynamically as needed\n- More flexible but can have higher latency for context switching\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static/dedicated CCS allocation**\n- CCS contexts are pre-allocated and assigned\n- More deterministic behavior\n- Generally preferred for most workloads, especially compute-heavy ones\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Disabled / no CCS handling**\n- Compute Slice functionality is effectively turned off\n- All work runs on the Graphics Slice (GCS)\n\n## When to use Mode 4\n\nYou'd typically use `ccs_mode=4` in these situations:\n\n1. **Debugging issues** \u2014 If you're experiencing crashes, hangs, or instability with compute workloads, disabling CCS can help isolate whether the CCS subsystem is the culprit.\n\n2. **Compatibility problems** \u2014 Some older or specific applications/drivers may have issues with CCS handling, and disabling it can serve as a workaround.\n\n3. **Testing** \u2014 When you want to benchmark or test the Graphics Slice in isolation.\n\n## Practical recommendation\n\nFor most users, **mode 2** is the sensible default for compute workloads (CUDA/Vulkan/OpenCL compute). Mode 1 may work fine for general use. Mode 4 is really a troubleshooting tool rather than a performance option.\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm fairly confident about the general direction of these modes, but the exact semantics of `ccs_mode` values can vary between driver versions and I'm not 100% certain of"}